    "id": "post_1791904172831",
    "type": "attraction",
    "title": "Keylong",
    "subtitle": "Complete Oasis of Lahaul Guide: Shashur & Kardang Monasteries, Best Time, Stay Options & Route via Atal Tunnel",
    "category": "Attraction",
    "author": "Team Trail Explorers",
    "author_avatar": "/assets/images/trips/img_1791903912498_3.png",
    "read_time": "8 min read",
    "image_url": "/assets/images/trips/img_1791904109770_Keylong__Himachal_Pradesh.jpg",
    "content": "Keylong (also spelled Kyelang) is the administrative headquarters and cultural capital of Lahaul and Spiti district in Himachal Pradesh. Perched at an altitude of 10,100 feet (3,080 meters) above sea level along the Bhaga River, this vibrant Himalayan settlement serves as the green, willow-fringed oasis between the lush valleys of Kullu and the barren high deserts of Ladakh and Zanskar.\n\nFollowing the opening of the Atal Tunnel, Keylong transitioned from a summer-only transit halt on the Leh-Manali Highway into a premier year-round destination known for ancient Tibetan Buddhist gompas, rugged mountain views, and local Lahauli culture.\n\n---\n\n### Quick Facts: Keylong at a Glance\n\n| Feature | Details |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **Altitude / Elevation** | 10,100 feet / 3,080 meters |\n| **Location** | Lahaul Valley, Himachal Pradesh |\n| **District Headquarters** | Lahaul & Spiti |\n| **Distance from Manali** | ~71 km via Atal Tunnel |\n| **Distance from Atal Tunnel (North Portal)** | ~43 km |\n| **Best Time to Visit** | May to October (Summer/Autumn) & Jan to Feb (Snow/Winter) |\n| **Nearest Fuel Station** | Tandi Petrol Pump (~8 km before Keylong) |\n| **Permits Required** | None for Indian travelers; standard ID verification at checkposts |\n\n---\n\n### Top Attractions & Things to Do in Keylong\n\nKeylong is surrounded by centuries-old Drukpa Kagyu monasteries and panoramic views of the Kardang ridge:\n\n- **Kardang Monastery (Gompa):** Situated directly opposite Keylong across the Bhaga River, Kardang is the historic seat of the Drukpa Kagyu order in Lahaul. Founded in the 12th century and renovated in 1912, it houses sacred thangkas, ancient Kangyur manuscripts, and monumental stupas.\n- **Shashur Monastery:** Perched on the hill right above Keylong, Shashur is encircled by blue pine forests. It is famous for its intricate wall paintings and the annual Chham (sacred mask dance) festival held in June/July.\n- **Tayul Gompa:** Located near Satingri (~4 km from Keylong), Tayul features a massive 12-foot statue of Guru Padmasambhava and one of the largest prayer wheels in the valley.\n- **The Confluence at Tandi:** Just 8 km south of Keylong lies Tandi, the sacred confluence where the Chandra and Bhaga rivers merge to form the Chandrabhaga (Chenab River).\n- **Lahaul Tribal Museum:** Located in the main market area, displaying traditional Lahauli attire, instruments, stone relics, and antique household utensils.\n\n---\n\n### Route Overview: How to Reach Keylong from Manali\n\n1. **Manali to South Portal (~28 km):** Head north via Solang Valley toward Dhundi.\n2. **Transit Atal Tunnel (9.02 km):** Cross the Pir Panjal range into the Chandra Valley at Sissu (North Portal).\n3. **Sissu to Tandi Confluence (~34 km):** Drive along the banks of the Chandra River past Gondhla, enjoying views of the historic Gondhla Fort tower.\n4. **Tandi to Keylong (~8 km):** Cross the bridge over the Bhaga River and ascend the switchbacks into Keylong town.\n\n---\n\n### Best Time to Visit Keylong\n\n- **May to June (Spring & Early Summer):** Clear blue skies, pleasant daytime temperatures (15\u00b0C to 22\u00b0C), melting snow peaks, and blooming apple orchards.\n- **July to August (Monsoon Period):** Lahaul falls in the rain-shadow zone and receives far less rain than Manali. Ideal for travelers heading toward Zanskar via Shinku La or Leh.\n- **September to October (Autumn Colors):** Crisp golden poplar and willow trees across the valley, dry mountain roads, and crystal-clear star-gazing nights.\n- **December to February (Winter Snow Expedition):** Keylong transforms into a quiet, snow-covered landscape. Excellent for winter adventure seekers, though daytime temperatures drop below freezing.\n\n---\n\n### Practical Travel Tips for Keylong\n\n- **Acclimatization Stopover:** Because Keylong sits at an accessible 10,100 feet, it is the safest acclimatization base to spend a night or two before crossing high passes like Baralacha La (16,040 ft) or Shinku La (16,580 ft).\n- **Fuel Availability:** The Tandi fuel station (8 km south) is the legendary last petrol pump on the Leh highway for nearly 365 km until Karu. Fill up your tank in Tandi before driving deeper into Lahaul, Zanskar, or Ladakh.\n- **Banking & ATMs:** Keylong has operational branches of SBI and PNB with working ATMs, though carrying emergency cash is still recommended during peak winter or bad weather.\n- **Connectivity:** 4G and 5G networks (Jio and Airtel) provide consistent internet coverage throughout Keylong town and surrounding villages.\n\n---\n\n### Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs)\n\n**Q1. How far is Keylong from Manali via the Atal Tunnel?**  \nKeylong is approximately 71 km from Manali. With the Atal Tunnel bypassing Rohtang Pass, the drive takes only 2 to 2.5 hours under standard road conditions.\n\n**Q2. Is a special permit required to visit Keylong?**  \nNo special permit is required for Indian tourists visiting Keylong or Lahaul Valley. Foreign tourists need standard valid travel visas and identification documents.\n\n**Q3. Does Keylong have good hotel and homestay facilities?**  \nYes. Keylong offers a wide selection of boutique hotels, HPTDC properties (Hotel Chandrabhaga), authentic Lahauli village homestays, and budget guesthouses with parking and Wi-Fi.\n\n**Q4. Is the road to Keylong open in winter?**  \nThanks to the Atal Tunnel, the route from Manali to Keylong remains largely accessible year-round, except during short periods of heavy snowfall when the Border Roads Organisation (BRO) clears the tarmac.",
